# LeakSanitizer suppressions — third-party zenoh-c runtime.
#
# `libzenohc.so` does not release the allocations its runtime makes when a
# `zenoh::Session` is dropped: the transport manager, the routing tables
# (`Network::new`, `HatCode::init`), the task controller and the plugin
# search dirs stay allocated in detached runtime threads. A test that opens a
# session and lets it go out of scope mid-run therefore ends with those blocks
# unreachable, and LSan reports them.
#
# Measured on the SCE side before adding this file (2026-07-31): 50 records,
# 22002 bytes in 118 allocations, all of them INDIRECT — zero direct leaks,
# i.e. LSan found no leaked root pointer held by SCE code. Adding an explicit
# `Session::close()` on every session the fixture opens left the numbers
# byte-for-byte identical, which rules out a missing-close on our side.
#
# The suppression is scoped to the offending shared object, so every
# allocation SCE itself makes stays under leak detection.
leak:libzenohc.so
